  "summary": "Regional variant of Mannerist architecture in Siena characterized by decorative richness, spatial complexity, and playful manipulation of classical forms.",
  "description": "Sienese Mannerism developed as a sophisticated response to Florentine Renaissance austerity, incorporating elaborate ornamentation and dramatic spatial effects while maintaining classical references. It reflected Siena's distinct artistic identity and rivalry with Florence during the late Renaissance period.",
  "culturalContext": "Sienese Mannerism — the Sienese school's late flourish after Baldassare Peruzzi and Perino del Vaga — ran Renaissance Siena's marble-and-brick craft into ornamental extremes: candelabra-flat facades, fanciful loggias (Loggia del Papa), terracotta-and-marble porticoes, and Peruzzi's stage-set spatial illusions. Peruzzi's drawings influenced Serlio and the general Mannerist grammar; Siena's bank palaces (Spannocchi, Tolomei) show the style's merchant-craft elegance against Florence's Tuscan bravura.",
